Install a berth binary from a local file as the teardown pin. You give its
sha256, and this approves exactly the bytes you hold: take the digest of the
binary itself (for a download, extract `berth` from the archive you verified,
then run `sha256sum berth`), and it is a meaningful check only when it comes
from a second trusted source, such as your own build. The file is read once,
those bytes are hashed and compared with it, and the same bytes are stored in
the control home. No signature is checked and nothing is run. There is one pin
per control home, and pinning again replaces it. The command shows what it will
pin and asks you to type a phrase, unless you pass --yes.

Usage:
  berth teardown pin --file PATH --sha256 HEX --release LABEL [flags]

Flags:
      --file string      the berth binary to pin (required)
  -h, --help             help for pin
      --release string   a label for this pin, such as the version you built
                         (required)
      --sha256 string    the sha256 of that file itself, from a source you trust
                         (required)
      --yes              skip the confirmation phrase

Global Flags:
      --config string   path to berth.toml (default: nearest above cwd)
  -V, --version         output the version number
